#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\cos x\n$$\n\nSo, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x\\cos x)' &= u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)' \\\\ & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x) \\\\ & = \\cos x - x \\sin x \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x$, find $f'(x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x$. So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime }(x) &= (x^{2})^{\\prime }\\ln x + x^{2}(\\ln x)^{\\prime } \\\\\n&= 2x \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n&= 2x\\ln x + x \\\\\n&= x(2\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sin{x}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x} \\cdot \\cos x + \\sin x \\cdot e^{x} \\\\\n&= e^{x}(\\cos x + \\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\ninto the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = \\pi /2} &= e^{\\pi /2}(\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\\n&= e^{\\pi /2}(0 + 1) \\\\\n&= e^{\\pi /2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3f1858a-d20a-565c-91b9-495c78563c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x+sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245910} {:answer/id #uuid "4301915f-34b8-5b2e-ad1b-db35916b7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245911} {:answer/id #uuid "6637e5fb-83b7-57d9-97ff-95c70f40cc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}cos⁡x+sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245912} {:answer/id #uuid "f33cbbcc-321e-5ce3-a167-b6e07d8a84ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245913} {:answer/id #uuid "8967477b-dab2-59b9-ba2c-21ef6d912ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x+{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4301915f-34b8-5b2e-ad1b-db35916b7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245911} :answer-field/id #uuid "932fa982-2a48-548f-bf5c-f3c9692b28ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a81ccd3e-892c-5b23-9c59-76ae09b82ed4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-104297" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}cos⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}cos⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(cos⁡x)+({e}^{x})(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fa68c9-3bb4-59ec-be8a-1ee57b90ff06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{csc}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245917} {:answer/id #uuid "fe287f89-f1a4-5fd6-aead-acbf238e88bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245918} {:answer/id #uuid "b000acc0-45e1-554b-8e26-e9cef32982df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}sec⁡x+{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245919} {:answer/id #uuid "d6405989-b549-5549-b1f2-4e0f27d5246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245920} {:answer/id #uuid "08e65ca7-34fb-5cb1-9e45-74398b142a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6405989-b549-5549-b1f2-4e0f27d5246f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245920} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5314495-ceb6-51f6-acdd-59565a81e6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245916}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5803ecfa-d65f-5c75-ac33-36f78f63aef0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-1931" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}tan⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (tan⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(tan⁡x)+({e}^{x})({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c6c61a9-4a9e-5eeb-9400-d216b97e222d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}+sin⁡xlog⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245924} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f89b35-a54e-5b89-bfd5-e57abaf54257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{x}-sin⁡xlog⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245925} {:answer/id #uuid "56900f49-fe53-55e2-9d06-0b76dd4c62a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245926} {:answer/id #uuid "20ce28b2-2d05-5eac-a2f5-25cb2927259c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{ln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245927} {:answer/id #uuid "c86f57a2-4d0e-54e6-9d72-8484497e5912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{x}-sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56900f49-fe53-55e2-9d06-0b76dd4c62a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245926} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e07e6cb-a349-52a4-9a7e-17ad6582cfcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9dc1699-3a43-566d-9a76-d728b892276f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-1934" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=cos⁡xlog⁡x,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=cos⁡x$ and $v(x)=log⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(cos⁡x)}^{′}log⁡x+cos⁡x{(log⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(-sin⁡x)(log⁡x)+(cos⁡x)(\\frac{1}{xln⁡10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3814b3a0-ffe0-5c94-9b15-20fdb1c1cd2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x(2-\\sin(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245931} {:answer/id #uuid "250caafe-64a7-5c39-b20f-9a8d8e88ebd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x(2+\\sin(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250caafe-64a7-5c39-b20f-9a8d8e88ebd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x(2+\\sin(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245932} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa588bab-7d10-5469-a986-a7030256b192" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245930}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67285904-f548-5015-a58f-0525bb936013" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319350" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)).$\n\n$({e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=2+sin⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}sec⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2+sin⁡(x))+({e}^{x})(cos⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(2+sin⁡(x)){e}^{x}+{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae5a149-0c74-5362-94d4-f1d2efbf8241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae5a149-0c74-5362-94d4-f1d2efbf8241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245935} :answer-field/id #uuid "a694e1ca-3d1f-5b77-bbec-f633c40dcf83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55c03f31-8ac1-5a56-a46f-8459d217f2ce" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319357" :question/problem "Differentiate $2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x).$\n\n$(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡(x)+2{x}^{2}\\cdot (cos⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(4x)(cos⁡(x))+(2{x}^{2})(-sin⁡(x)) \\\\  & =4xcos⁡(x)-2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ee957e-1b51-5604-aa79-567e37a99ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}\\sec(x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ee957e-1b51-5604-aa79-567e37a99ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}\\sec(x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245938} :answer-field/id #uuid "240812b4-90d5-5a54-bc15-81b785846fe7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245937}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5331b913-1534-51bc-898a-115837523a77" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319385" :question/problem "Differentiate $\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x).$\n\n$(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′}=\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡(x)+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (tan⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}(tan⁡(x))+(\\sqrt{x})({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007d6d89-e391-5bca-ab0c-fed8cee73035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4^x\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245941} {:answer/id #uuid "3deb2f4e-c7cb-5adf-89f2-459e32dc25ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4^x\\sec(x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3deb2f4e-c7cb-5adf-89f2-459e32dc25ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4^x\\sec(x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245942} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c3a932-6fda-5822-8d61-4f184e97290c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245940}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b6ecc2c-4a26-5a15-81e5-8545e9ddf3a8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319425" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}tan⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{(tan⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}ln⁡(4))tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af56b612-6a45-559f-b0e4-339ceb6e3c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af56b612-6a45-559f-b0e4-339ceb6e3c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2bc35eb-cb18-53a6-a664-4316b59dd9ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "341d8268-3e52-5e9c-8d92-5fa8fdd54c6d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319806" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x+2)cos⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-sin⁡x)+cos⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)sin⁡x+cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)sin⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+cos⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b1edfd-df36-5ac5-82a0-e7771a6bc79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245948} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cd0e5-6d6f-5a22-a31e-95b1933fe74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cd0e5-6d6f-5a22-a31e-95b1933fe74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245949} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6fa0ec-97c7-5d07-9255-9a82b7afae8b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "119e9e4c-c48a-58b7-ad3b-e8e76b4e81f2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319829" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d18b49a-29f3-5d21-a01f-2181aca28330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d18b49a-29f3-5d21-a01f-2181aca28330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245952} :answer-field/id #uuid "b588f616-32bc-5294-80e6-7534a387f08c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245951}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4919c5bf-bc12-5af9-8e7c-1d76c4c20ab5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319842" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{2}-4x+7){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}-4x+7$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+7)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0-0+3)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908f5813-24e4-5722-af75-7bfad4c49afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3xe^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245955} {:answer/id #uuid "95cd1d02-1ad4-5316-bf53-0b4526bf9bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95cd1d02-1ad4-5316-bf53-0b4526bf9bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245956} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce5e0fc-e7ef-53ec-b7d1-4b67a28f0f43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245954}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d339bf7-2068-5f66-88da-f338ed057e57" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319902" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{x}^{3}{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{3}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({e}^{x})+({x}^{3})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}+{x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117262d1-644f-51d5-b18e-d7e0cc95d46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "117262d1-644f-51d5-b18e-d7e0cc95d46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5099b38-c89e-5815-aeff-ff7eba0b6d69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245958}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48f201c1-c07d-592c-bdf0-148678ba4e79" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-319903" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=10x{e}^{x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=5{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(5{x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+5{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(10x)({e}^{x})+(5{x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =10x{e}^{x}+5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e285f04-b30b-5d66-821a-6e3df0108e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5^{-x}\\ln(5)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(x)}{\\ln(7)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e285f04-b30b-5d66-821a-6e3df0108e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5^{-x}\\ln(5)\\left(\\frac{\\ln(x)}{\\ln(7)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245962} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9a2335-1879-5d6c-89b4-4d94cc13d496" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24eceaef-2370-5c05-b195-43f3fecf97c8" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-320816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{-x}{log}_{7}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={5}^{-x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({5}^{-x})}^{′}{log}_{7}⁡(x)+{5}^{-x}{({log}_{7}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5))({log}_{7}⁡(x))+({5}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{xln⁡(7)}) \\\\  & =-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5){log}_{7}⁡(x)+\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "508da6c4-b80d-54e7-bbbb-695d79fd2e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245965} {:answer/id #uuid "17796f93-2d45-52ff-95fe-cbe9dd8c4323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi {e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245966} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc950ac-a57c-5229-91d5-b99f45effd19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245967} {:answer/id #uuid "49cef95d-4239-5d30-99ec-56044816e650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245968} {:answer/id #uuid "92af597c-f699-54ab-9af9-37d43ec79519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "508da6c4-b80d-54e7-bbbb-695d79fd2e33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245965} :answer-field/id #uuid "66567d8c-da1a-5a49-8192-4b1ed8507244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245964}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc9975f3-0886-5dd3-9861-8f258d2d4517" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48516" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y={e}^{x}sin⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=sin⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+(sin⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & ={{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06776ac1-2b07-5ea5-b424-d4df98dec91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245972} {:answer/id #uuid "c2073118-60a1-5cd7-a2fd-2d7e050adeb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245973} {:answer/id #uuid "235b4182-f195-5f0f-af5f-ef3319f6ecf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245974} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0fd00-a479-5d89-97fc-630e69d9fd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245975} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b2ac9e-ba6e-5ae8-b082-88cb3a08b53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2073118-60a1-5cd7-a2fd-2d7e050adeb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245973} :answer-field/id #uuid "5195554f-a9d7-59e7-a999-266765561a9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245971}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8b5cdf9-a868-5624-a5a7-6b797578fa7a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48993" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=xln⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=ln⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(ln⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+ln⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=e} & =1+ln⁡x \\\\  & =1+ln⁡e \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2.$"}]}
